From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Koushick Varun <51849493+kvarun-p@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 00:48:01 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] nvkmd/macos: no host-visible VRAM; nvkmapvram for NVK's
 own buffers

A CPU mapping of VRAM (through BAR1) can't be revoked once a process has
duplicated it, so the kext never reuses that BAR1 range and caps how much
can be held. Offer no host-visible VRAM memory type (nor the BAR-sized
heap) on macOS: memory the CPU maps comes from system memory.

Boot-arg nvkmapvram (or NVK_MACOS_MAPVRAM for one process) puts some of
NVK's own LOCAL|CAN_MAP buffers back in CPU-mapped VRAM, which saves the
GPU PCIe reads per dispatch: "1"/"all", or a list of "desc" (descriptor
pools and tables) and "cmd"/"cmd:<n>" (command chunks, at most n per
device). They are mapped at allocation, so once the kext's cap is
reached they come from system memory instead of failing. New
NVKMD_MEM_USE_CMD/USE_DESC placement hints tell the backend what a
buffer is for; other backends ignore them.

llama.cpp tg, Qwen2.5 0.5B: 97 t/s none, ~101 desc, 114 all.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
